1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,288
Welcome to Absolute ADAS technician training.

2
00:00:03,638 --> 00:00:12,406
This one covers the steering column inspection that General Motors requires after a collision, using a 2020 GM C Sierra as the example.

3
00:00:12,756 --> 00:00:20,403
The numbers you'll see are examples from one truck. The method is what matters: look up the procedure for the exact vehicle in front of you.

4
00:00:22,003 --> 00:00:29,843
After a collision, the vehicle has to go back to factory spec. The manufacturer publishes exactly which parts must be inspected or replaced.

5
00:00:30,193 --> 00:00:33,697
Some of that guidance is loosely worded. Some of it is black and white.

6
00:00:34,047 --> 00:00:39,983
A steering column is designed to collapse in a crash. It can be damaged with nothing visible from the driver's seat.

7
00:00:40,333 --> 00:00:46,605
So if the inspection is required and nobody does it, the repair isn't complete, even if the truck drives fine.

8
00:00:48,205 --> 00:00:50,189
Two things decide what's required.

9
00:00:50,539 --> 00:00:58,827
First, how severe was the collision? Cosmetic damage only, damage beyond cosmetic without airbag deployment, or airbag deployment.

10
00:00:59,177 --> 00:01:07,385
Second, how was this exact vehicle built? The VIN and the RPO option codes tell you which procedure and which specifications apply.

11
00:01:08,985 --> 00:01:11,449
Step one. Look it up by VIN.

12
00:01:11,799 --> 00:01:15,480
Enter the VIN in ALLDATA, or in GM service information.

13
00:01:15,829 --> 00:01:20,277
Go to Body and Frame, then Repairs and Inspections Required After a Collision.

14
00:01:20,628 --> 00:01:28,787
ALLDATA pulls from GM, but it sometimes has gaps. If something you expect isn't there, check GM service information directly.

15
00:01:30,387 --> 00:01:32,723
Step two. Match the damage to the chart.

16
00:01:33,073 --> 00:01:37,441
The page is laid out like a checklist, with a column for each level of severity.

17
00:01:37,792 --> 00:01:48,256
The middle level is damage that exceeds minor outer body panel cosmetic distortion, without airbag deployment. Think more than dents and scratches: a bent bracket, frame rail, or support.

18
00:01:48,605 --> 00:01:57,197
At that level the list is already long. It includes the steering wheel airbag, the steering wheel, the steering column, and the impact sensors.

19
00:01:57,548 --> 00:02:05,067
Where it says inspect the steering column, it sends you to the steering column accident damage inspection procedure. That's the one we're doing here.

20
00:02:06,667 --> 00:02:09,115
Step three. Find the RPO codes.

21
00:02:09,465 --> 00:02:17,385
The procedure asks two questions: does the truck have a front floor console, and does it have RPO code N33 or N37?

22
00:02:17,735 --> 00:02:23,543
RPO codes are three-character factory option codes. They tell you which steering column was installed.

23
00:02:23,893 --> 00:02:31,413
Pull the build sheet by VIN. One free option is repair link shop dot com. Then press Ctrl+F and search for each code.

24
00:02:31,763 --> 00:02:37,347
Our example truck has no floor console, and it has N37, a tilt and telescoping column.

25
00:02:38,947 --> 00:02:40,627
Here's why the code matters.

26
00:02:40,977 --> 00:02:46,641
In this example, the minimum overall length for the N37 column is 506 mm.

27
00:02:46,991 --> 00:02:50,479
For the N33 column, it's 530 mm.

28
00:02:50,829 --> 00:02:57,517
That's twenty-4 mm apart. Pick the wrong code, and a damaged column can pass, or a good one can fail.

29
00:02:59,117 --> 00:03:00,973
Step four. Remove the column.

30
00:03:01,323 --> 00:03:05,819
Follow the manufacturer's removal procedure, including the airbag safety steps.

31
00:03:06,169 --> 00:03:12,329
The column has to come out and be stripped down to the bare column. That's the only way to get an accurate end to end measurement.

32
00:03:12,679 --> 00:03:17,671
Before you measure, set the column fully telescoped in, which means fully collapsed.

33
00:03:19,271 --> 00:03:20,791
Step five. Measure.

34
00:03:21,141 --> 00:03:25,669
There are three checks. Use the manufacturer's illustration for the exact points.

35
00:03:26,019 --> 00:03:33,139
One: the upper shaft end, where the steering wheel bolts on. In our example it must not be less than 58 mm.

36
00:03:33,489 --> 00:03:40,465
Two: the overall length, end to end. For our example truck it must not be less than 506 mm.

37
00:03:40,815 --> 00:03:49,215
Three: with the tilt lever unlocked, move the column in the radial and axial directions. In our example, the movement must not be more than 4 mm.

38
00:03:49,565 --> 00:03:54,077
Measure in millimeters, with a tram gauge or a digital tape, and write each number down.

39
00:03:55,677 --> 00:03:57,549
Step six. Pass or fail.

40
00:03:57,899 --> 00:04:05,371
If any measurement is out of spec, the column is damaged. Discard it and install a new one. It doesn't get repaired or reused.

41
00:04:05,721 --> 00:04:10,489
If everything is in spec, reinstall the column following the manufacturer's procedure.

42
00:04:10,839 --> 00:04:15,879
If you're not sure where to measure or how to read the spec, stop and ask before you decide.

43
00:04:17,480 --> 00:04:19,032
Step seven. Document it.

44
00:04:19,382 --> 00:04:24,230
Save the procedure pages for this VIN, and the build sheet showing the RPO code.

45
00:04:24,579 --> 00:04:29,779
Take a photo of each measurement with the tape visible, and record the numbers and the result in the job file.

46
00:04:30,129 --> 00:04:36,274
Keep it with the pre-scan and post-scan reports. That's the proof the inspection was required and was done.

47
00:04:37,874 --> 00:04:43,153
To recap. Look up the VIN and open Repairs and Inspections Required After a Collision.

48
00:04:43,504 --> 00:04:48,192
Match the severity, then find the RPO code so you're on the right procedure.

49
00:04:48,541 --> 00:04:52,685
Remove, strip, and collapse the column. Take the three measurements.

50
00:04:53,036 --> 00:04:57,708
Out of spec, replace it. In spec, reinstall it. Document it either way.

51
00:04:58,057 --> 00:05:04,298
And never reuse numbers from another vehicle, or from this video. Always pull the spec for the VIN you're working on.
